@pod
Feature: The pod context is generated from the relation types
  A pod names what its keys mean in two context files. papyrus.context.jsonld is generated by Papyrus and never edited by hand: its relation terms
  come from papyrus-semantic-relation-types.yml, where each relation type that a pod may use is marked `pod: true`, and its plain keys and link keys
  are the profile's own. It records the profile version and the Vault-LD version it pins, so a new Vault-LD release changes nothing until the pin is moved on
  purpose. The newsroom's bookkeeping relation types never appear in it. context.jsonld is the owner's file: the id base and the site's own keys.

  Scenario: Only the relation types marked for pods become terms
    Given a relation-types file in which "tagged_with" is marked for pods and "quality_rating_is" is not
    When the pod context is generated from that file
    Then the context defines "tagged_with" as a link to the note it names
    And the context does not mention "quality_rating_is"

  Scenario: The shipped relation-types file marks the knowledge relations for pods
    When the pod context is generated from the shipped relation types
    Then the relation terms of the context are exactly:
      """
      mentions
      authored_by
      cites
      about
      derived_from
      related_to
      tagged_with
      see_also
      same_as
      broader_than
      narrower_than
      member_of
      supports
      contradicts
      """

  Scenario: The context pins the profile and Vault-LD
    When the pod context is generated from the shipped relation types
    Then the context records profile version 1, Vault-LD 0.5.0 and commit "025e71b"

  Scenario: The context says which classes a note may have
    When the pod context is generated from the shipped relation types
    Then the context lists the classes "papyrus:Article", "papyrus:Post", "papyrus:Page", "papyrus:Reference", "papyrus:Concept", "papyrus:Person", "papyrus:Organization", "papyrus:Section" and "papyrus:Relation"

  Scenario: The profile has no lifecycle keys yet
    When the pod context is generated from the shipped relation types
    Then the context defines none of "stage", "rating", "reviewedBy", "reviewedOn", "reviewedHash", "spikedReason", "heldReason"

  Scenario: Generating twice gives the same bytes
    When the pod context is generated from the shipped relation types
    And the pod context is generated again
    Then the two contexts are byte for byte the same

  Scenario: The fixture pod carries the generated context unchanged
    Given a copy of the fixture pod
    Then the pod file "papyrus.context.jsonld" is exactly the context Papyrus generates
